# Uncompressed edge WebAssembly assets

Status: implementation and local validation in progress; no asset upload or
deployment is authorized by this record.

The existing Bunny bundler invokes gzip on the LibSass and Photon binaries.
Compression is prohibited by the operator's current storage/build rules. A
real esbuild preflight without that plugin exceeds the existing boot-safe
script budget. The budget remains enforced; removing the guard would not
establish a working runtime.

Keep all JavaScript in one script, preserving decision 0009's rejection of
remote JavaScript chunk loading and unverified import behavior. Emit only the
two existing raw WebAssembly binaries as separately hashed deployment assets.
The build requires an explicit HTTPS asset-base URL and emits a manifest with
each source, byte length, SHA-256, license and exact asset URL. It performs no
upload, fetch or compression. Deployment requires the exact script/assets/
hosting package, integrity readback and approved private-copy validation.
Build into a new empty output directory; refusal precedes overwriting any
existing package. The manifest is written only after both scripts and raw
asset/license readback checks succeed. Preserve failed partial builds as
diagnostics rather than treating them as deployable packages.

Read-only requests must not fetch or initialize these binaries. The first
stylesheet compilation or image operation loads its required binary, refuses
redirects and credentials, bounds the response by the expected byte count,
checks SHA-256 before compilation, and reports a named failure. Concurrent
initialization shares its pending work. A failed transport or integrity check
must not poison an ECMAScript module's evaluation cache: a subsequent real
operation can retry. No automatic retry loop is added. A verified Photon
initialization failure remains visible rather than resetting third-party state
without proof that such a reset is safe.

This introduces an explicit availability dependency for write-side compilation
and image processing, and up to two initial asset subrequests per isolate.
Missing assets must fail the affected operation without silently storing
unprocessed output. Static registry/source-snapshot fallbacks remain embedded.
Native Node and other edge builds keep their existing vendored binary paths.

Verification covers exact raw-asset bytes, no request during module evaluation,
concurrent first use, bad status/redirect/length/digest, interrupted download,
later-operation recovery, and real LibSass/image processing through the built
artifact. Provider boot and network behavior require separate installed-copy
evidence; local Node execution is not Bunny acceptance. This build work does
not establish image-encoder byte parity with ImageMagick or full port parity.

## Local implementation evidence

The production bundler now uses the raw-binary plugin and requires
`--wasm-asset-base`; it no longer invokes the gzip plugin. The build file's
line endings were normalized to LF while removing that path. Both generated
scripts, raw assets, license files and their integrity manifest are emitted
without upload. Package overwrite refusal runs before artifact writes.

Eight executed tests across three files pass: descriptor admission and download
failures; actual full CLI packaging and overwrite refusal; real bundled LibSass
and Photon processors after failed status/digest responses; concurrent first
use; exact equality with the local vendored compiler/image processors; and the
existing finite Ruby image fixture's dimension/pixel comparison. Test transforms
may use the existing cache; assertions execute. Earlier seven-test invocations
and the TypeScript declaration/Buffer typing failures remain recorded; they are
not silently converted into a clean first run. No model judges these checks.

A dirty-source package built with the fixture host has full/read scripts of
6,115,660 and 2,190,781 UTF-8 bytes, respectively, below the unchanged 7,600,000
byte build guard. The raw binaries are 1,672,020 and 1,566,855 bytes, read back
with their SHA-256 and copied license bytes. Actual emitted scripts evaluated
in Node with only SDK server registration stubbed; each registered one handler
and made zero fetch calls. That diagnostic package predates the subsequent
signup privacy correction and is not an activation candidate or Bunny boot
measurement. Rebuild from the final frozen source before any installation.

Default synthetic-v1 byte observations pass diagnostically on the dirty
development source under N1/N2/N3, with no declared divergence. This does not
exercise remote asset hosting. The platform scan retains 20 preexisting
unguarded findings tracked in issue 48; none points to this raw-asset change.
Native-provider behavior, an independent source/package challenge, the exact
approved storage URL, asset upload/readback and activation remain outstanding.
The consuming-project coordinator owns the storage/pull-zone choice and has
instructed that fixture URLs be used until review; no original-site asset path
is assumed or accessed.

The independent runtime review challenged direct untyped JavaScript calls:
array/object URL and SHA-256 values were coerced by URL/regex operations,
allowing a request before eventual failure (or acceptance for a coercible URL).
Generated literal descriptors were not shown affected. The runtime boundary
now requires scalar URL/hash strings and snapshots all descriptor fields once
before parsing, fetching or comparing integrity. Getter failures refuse with a
named configuration error. The exported build-base parser likewise refuses
non-string values before URL coercion. New negative controls include arrays,
URL/boxed/coercible objects, malformed byte counts and a throwing getter, with
zero fetches, plus a valid descriptor whose getters are each read once.
Independent correction review remains required; the original adverse results
are retained by the reviewer rather than overwritten.
